From bf34bfc363d43d8dedd8731ad49b645f91456559 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 00:23:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make the opponent vary, think, and let you retry
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three complaints, one root each.

"Every time I practice the Sicilian it's always the same thing."
pickBookMove ended in .sort(byWeight)[0] — the single heaviest continuation,
every time, so a drill replayed one identical game. It now draws at random,
weighted by weight squared so main lines still dominate, with anything under a
twentieth of the top move dropped as a novelty (without that floor a Sicilian
drill answers 1...c5 with 2.Nh3). Over 400 draws White plays 2.Nf3 397 times
and the drill still reaches Najdorf, Dragon, Classical and Closed structures
instead of one fixed line.

"As soon as I move the computer plays within 0.1 seconds."
Two separate causes. A fixed-depth search returns the same move from the same
position every time, so the opponent was a recording; and at depth 4 it
returned in about 100ms. Levels now carry `choices` and `spread`: the engine
searches MultiPV, and plays a weighted pick from everything within `spread`
centipawns of its favourite. Weaker levels choose from a wider, worse pool,
which is what a weaker human does. Full strength keeps `spread: 0` and stays
deterministic. Levels also carry a `think` range and engineMove spends the
remainder of it after the search returns, so a reply lands in 0.7-2.2s at Club
rather than instantly.

"When I train I want to do the move and if I have it wrong do it again. If I
have it I want the computer to keep playing."
The trainer graded one attempt, printed the answer, and froze the board. Now a
wrong move says what it costs and puts the position back for another go —
the answer is only revealed if you ask for it. The spaced-repetition card is
still graded on the FIRST attempt, so retrying to understand a position never
inflates the schedule. A right move hands the position back and the engine
plays on from there, so you convert what you just found instead of collecting
a tick.

Engine.evalPosition gained a `multipv` option and returns `moves`, every
candidate the search kept, alongside the existing top-line fields.

